daemon: clamp the -0x107 "force update write address" to never regress
buf_wr_index behind buf_head_index

Depends on 0013 (numbered after it; same function, dev_dat_so_write_proc()).

Background: continuing to root-cause the SDIO-mode throughput stalls
(see this project's ar8030-transport repo, README's "SDIO chardev"
section) after 0013/0014 measurably reduced their frequency, a tight
(sub-second-granularity) live capture of the daemon's own debug-pad log
caught a *hard*, non-recovering lockup (throughput pinned at 0Mbit,
every subsequent bb_socket_write() timing out, never self-recovering)
in the act. The log showed sock_dev_push_data()'s own sanity-check
warning ("buf_wr_index = 0x33aa6c6, buf_head_index = 0x33aceab") firing
repeatedly with buf_head_index *greater* than buf_wr_index -- an
inverted state that should be structurally impossible, since
buf_head_index (confirmed-received) should never exceed buf_wr_index
(dispatched-so-far).

Once inverted, sock_dev_need_write()'s own
`(uint32_t)(psock->buf_wr_index - psock->buf_head_index)` computation
underflows (unsigned subtraction, negative operand) to a huge bogus
offset, which it passes straight to ringbuffer_get_data() as the "how
far into the ring buffer is unsent data" offset -- that call then
(correctly, given the bogus input) finds nothing there and reports
"nothing to send", permanently, for this socket, no matter how much
genuinely new data (visible via add_new_wr_node()'s own logging) piles
up behind it. Every other site that writes buf_wr_index (the normal
pack->sta>=0 ack path in this same function, and the -0x108
"force resend" path just below this one) explicitly guards against ever
setting it below buf_head_index; the -0x107 "force update write
address" handler was the one exception, accepting the chip-reported
retmsg.pos completely unguarded. The capture showed a burst of dozens
of -0x107 messages in a handful of milliseconds, all reporting the same
already-superseded pos (behind buf_head_index) -- whatever exactly
causes the chip to emit that burst, this daemon has no business trusting
a report that contradicts data it has already confirmed received.

Fix: clamp -- only accept retmsg.pos if it's not behind buf_head_index,
otherwise use buf_head_index itself (a no-op update, restoring the
invariant instead of violating it further).

Confirmed on real hardware: this specific hard-lockup signature (the
inverted buf_wr_index/buf_head_index pair, frozen, in
sock_dev_push_data()'s warning log) has not been reproduced since. A
second, distinct hard-lockup mechanism remains open, *not* fixed by
this patch -- see the README's own writeup for the live evidence: the
chip's own -0x108 wanted_pos can apparently freeze at a stale value
already behind buf_head_index (so this daemon correctly declines to act
on it, per the guard 0013 already added) while its own "written pos"
keeps climbing, and nothing else exists to flush the genuinely-new
backlog that piles up once that happens -- possibly a chip-firmware-side
behavior rather than something fixable purely in this daemon's own
bookkeeping. Keep digging there; this patch narrows the search but does
not close it.

--- a/daemon/sock_node.c
+++ b/daemon/sock_node.c
@@ -116,7 +116,26 @@
         } break;
         case -0x107: {
             // 强制更新写入地址
-            psock->buf_wr_index = retmsg.pos;
+            /* Confirmed on real hardware (0015-*.patch): retmsg.pos here is
+             * a position reported *by the chip*, with no guarantee it's
+             * still ahead of what this daemon has already confirmed
+             * received (psock->buf_head_index) -- a stale/out-of-order
+             * -0x107 can report a pos *behind* buf_head_index. Every other
+             * site that writes buf_wr_index (the normal pack->sta>=0 ack
+             * path, the -0x108 force-resend path) maintains buf_wr_index
+             * >= buf_head_index as an invariant; this was the one
+             * exception, accepting retmsg.pos completely unguarded. Once
+             * buf_wr_index < buf_head_index, sock_dev_need_write()'s own
+             * (uint32_t)(buf_wr_index - buf_head_index) computation
+             * underflows to a huge bogus offset, permanently breaking its
+             * ringbuffer_get_data() "is there anything to send" check for
+             * this socket -- observed live as a hard, non-recovering
+             * lockup (throughput pinned at 0, every subsequent write
+             * timing out) once a burst of stale -0x107 messages (all
+             * reporting the same already-superseded pos) pinned
+             * buf_wr_index below buf_head_index. Clamping here restores
+             * the invariant every other write site already relies on. */
+            psock->buf_wr_index = (retmsg.pos >= psock->buf_head_index) ? retmsg.pos : psock->buf_head_index;
             com_log(COM_SOCKET_DATA,
                     "%d-%d send pending pos %#" PRIx64 ", len %" PRIx32 " , buf head pos = %#" PRIx64
                     " wr head pos = %#" PRIx64,
